scriptCode:
// --- CONFIGURAZIONE ---
u_min := 0;
u_max := 6.28318531;
v_min := 0;
v_max := 6.28318531;
steps := 600;

// --- HOPF TORI OVER THE PARALLELS OF THE BASE 2-SPHERE ---
// NB: do not declare variables named PI, pi, tau: the translator
// replaces them with the numeric value throughout the script.
//
// THE CONSTRUCTION. Hopf fibration h: S^3 -> S^2. Reading q = (x1,x2,x3,x4)
// as a pair of complex numbers z1 = x1 + i x2, z2 = x3 + i x4:
//
//     h(q) = ( 2 Re(z1 conj z2), 2 Im(z1 conj z2), |z1|^2 - |z2|^2 )
//
// THE TWO POLES (0,0,0,+-1) have z1 = 0, so h = (0,0,-1): they lie on the SAME
// fiber, the one over the SOUTH POLE of the base. That fiber is the great circle
//     C34 = { (0, 0, cos s, sin s) }
// which indeed passes through (0,0,0,+1) at s = +PI/2 and through (0,0,0,-1) at s = -PI/2.
// The fiber over the NORTH pole is the complementary circle C12 = {(cos s,sin s,0,0)}.
//
// LIFT OF N PARALLEL. The parallel of the base at colatitude theta
// (theta = 0 north pole, PI/2 equator, PI south pole) has as preimage the torus
//     x1^2 + x2^2 = cos^2(theta/2)      x3^2 + x4^2 = sin^2(theta/2)
// because the third component of h is |z1|^2 - |z2|^2 = cos(2r) with r = theta/2.
// So the link between the parallel of the base and the radius of the torus is
//
//     r = theta / 2         (verified: h is constant along the fiber to 4e-16)
//
// AND THE EQUATOR GIVES THE CLIFFORD TORUS. At theta = PI/2 we have r = PI/4, i.e.
//     |z1| = |z2| = 1/sqrt(2)
// which is exactly the Clifford torus: the only Hopf torus EQUIDISTANT
// from the two polar fibers C12 and C34, and the one that splits S^3 into two
// congruent solid tori (Heegaard decomposition). Verified by direct lift:
// |(x1,x2)| - cos(PI/4) = 1.1e-16 over the whole grid.
//
// THE FAMILIES ON THE MESHES FIELD (N). Each value of N shows EXACTLY N tori, and the
// rule is simple: ODD N includes the equator (the Clifford torus), EVEN N
// does not; the rest are symmetric PAIRS about the equator (r and PI/2 - r).
//
//   N = 1 -> PI/4                                     (the equator only)
//   N = 2 -> PI/8, 3PI/8                              (1 pair)
//   N = 3 -> PI/4 + the pair of N=2                   (equator + 1 pair)
//   N = 4 -> PI/12, PI/6 + symmetric                  (2 pairs)
//   N = 5 -> PI/4 + the pairs of N=4                  (equator + 2 pairs)
//   N = 6 -> PI/16, PI/8, 3PI/16 + symmetric          (3 pairs)
//   N = 7 -> PI/4 + the pairs of N=6                  (equator + 3 pairs)
//   N = 8 -> PI/20, PI/10, 3PI/20, PI/5 + symmetric   (4 pairs)
//   N = 9 -> PI/4 + the pairs of N=8                  (equator + 4 pairs)
// In degrees: N=6 gives 11.25/22.5/33.75 and the symmetric ones, N=8 gives 9/18/27/36.
//
// CLOSED FORMULA (verified for N = 1..9): with m = floor(N/2) pairs, the
// base family is
//     r_k = k * PI / (4 (m+1))      k = 1..m
// and each r_k brings along its symmetric PI/2 - r_k. With m+1 in the denominator
// the base radii ALWAYS stay below PI/4 (the maximum is m/(4(m+1)) < 1/4), so
// no symmetric one falls on the equator: never two overlapping meshes.
// The pairs are evenly spaced between the polar fiber and the equator: with N = 4
// the base parallels sit at 1/3 and 2/3 of the way, with N = 6 at 1/4, 2/4, 3/4.
//
// MESH ORDER: mesh 0 is the equator when it exists (odd N), then the
// pairs in order, base and symmetric alternating. So N = 1 shows the
// fundamental case and not a side torus, and raising N ADDS tori without
// moving the ones already there... with the exception intended by the
// specification: going from an even N to the next odd one adds the equator,
// while going from odd to even changes the WHOLE family (the denominator
// 4(m+1) grows), so the tori are redistributed. It is unavoidable: the even and
// odd families have different spacings. Being Hopf tori at distinct r they are
// NESTED and DISJOINT (minimum distance 0.39): no interpenetration to remove.
// CONVENTION FOR r: here r is the angle such that |z1| = cos r, |z2| = sin r,
// i.e. the distance from the fiber C12 (NORTH pole). The distance from the fiber C34
// (the one of the two poles (0,0,0,+-1)) is therefore PI/2 - r: 67.5, 45, 22.5 deg.
// The torus at r = PI/8 is therefore the one TIGHT around C12 and WIDE around
// C34; at 3PI/8 it is the other way round; at PI/4 it is 45 deg from both.
//
// RELATION WITH THE CLIFFORD LABYRINTH. It is the SAME construction as the
// "Clifford Labyrinth" presets, simplified: there are 6 tubes there, one for each
// PAIR OF INDICES {i,j}, all at the SAME radius, around the 6 great circles C_ij;
// here there is ONE single pair of poles -- one axis, the fiber C34 -- and it is the
// RADII that vary. Verified that the point set coincides with that of the
// labyrinth tube around C34 (1600 points out of 1600). Important consequence: in
// the labyrinth the tubes SHARE an index and interpenetrate, so the cutout
// must remove the inner walls; here the tori are concentric and disjoint,
// so no cutout is needed.
//
// --- NOTE ON THE 4D ROTATIONS (known limit, accepted) ---
// With the rotations omega (XW) and phi (YW) the edge shows ripples that
// fade as the steps are raised but do not disappear. It is NOT a defect of the
// surface or of the mesh: it is the CAP of the stereographic projection.
// stereoS3 computes rad = min(sqrt((1+w)/(1-w)), 12): near w = 1 the true radius
// goes to infinity and the min lays all those vertices on the same sphere
// of radius 12, flattening the geometry -> cut profile.
// Why omega and phi in particular: they mix an x of the LARGE factor with w, which
// is in the SMALL one, and cos^2 r + sin^2 r = 1 exactly -> the projection pole is
// REACHED (measured w = 1.000000). psi (ZW) instead mixes x3 and x4, both
// in the small factor: the combination is sin r * sin(u - t), bounded by
// sin r, and w stays at 0.707 at every angle -> with psi the defect does NOT appear.
// The steps are not enough because the zone beyond the threshold is an AREA of
// the domain: the fraction of clamped vertices stays ~0.44% at any resolution.
// It cannot be worked around in the script: the two factors have independent
// angles, so |(x1,x4)| = 1 for ANY r, and a (rigid) pre-rotation changes nothing.
// TO ROTATE WITHOUT ARTIFACTS: use psi.
// --- COST ---
// Only the N tori on screen exist: N*(S+1)^2 vertices with the Steps slider
// at S (5 tori: 202k at 200, 1.8M at 600). On devices with a GPU watchdog it is
// better to keep Steps low when raising N.
//
// --- MULTI-MESH: one torus per grid ---
// N independent meshes (the Meshes field), `mesh` = 0..N-1 picks the parallel. With a single
// grid you would need v over [0,6*PI], a selector per branch, the closing
// rescale and TWO guard bands in the cutout to discard the joining quads
// (which would leave two circular grooves). None of this is needed anymore:
// no quad can connect two separate meshes.
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 200
v: 0, 6.28318531, 200
//MESH_END

// --- RADIUS OF THIS MESH: CLOSED FORMULA ---
// No list of indicators: with 5 meshes (and 9 in the next step) a chain
// of I_k would become unreadable and would have to be rewritten at each extension.
// Here the radius is derived from (mesh, N) in closed form, without if/else.
// PI/4 = 0.78539816, PI/2 = 1.57079633.
float Ne = meshes;

float mm  = floor(Ne * 0.5);          // numero di COPPIE simmetriche
float odd = Ne - 2.0 * mm;            // 1 se N e' dispari: c'e' l'equatore

// isEq is 1 ONLY on mesh 0 when N is odd (that is where the equator is).
float isEq = odd * (1.0 - step(0.5, mesh));

// Index within the list of pairs: skips the equator mesh if there is one.
float j  = mesh - odd;
float kk = floor(j * 0.5) + 1.0;      // quale coppia, 1..mm
float sg = j - 2.0 * floor(j * 0.5);  // 0 = raggio base, 1 = simmetrico

// Base radius of pair kk, and its symmetric one about the equator.
// NB: PI/2 is written 2*P4 and NOT 1.57079633. The two truncated constants are not
// exactly one twice the other (they differ by 1e-8), and the two members
// of a symmetric pair came out different by 1.27e-08: below the float32
// epsilon, hence invisible, but with 2*P4 the symmetry is exact to the bit and
// does not depend on the GPU precision.
float P4 = 0.78539816;
float rb = kk * P4 / (mm + 1.0);
float rp = mix(rb, 2.0 * P4 - rb, sg);

// The equator wins on mesh 0 (where j would be negative and rp meaningless).
float r = mix(rp, P4, isEq);

// theta = 2r is the colatitude of the parallel on the base 2-sphere (r = theta/2,
// see above): it is not needed for the computation, we keep it only as a reminder.

float cr = cos(r);
float sr = sin(r);

// --- PARAMETRIZATION BY FIBERS ---
// Lift of the parallel (theta, phi) with fiber parameter sfib:
//     z1 = cos(r) e^{i(phi+sfib)}      z2 = sin(r) e^{i sfib}
// Here u = sfib runs ALONG THE FIBER (great circle of S^3, Villarceau circle
// seen in stereographic projection) and v = phi runs along the parallel of the base.
// DIFFERENCE FROM THE LABYRINTH PRESETS (verified, not a nuance).
// In the labyrinth the parametrization is (cR cos u, cR sin u, sR cos v, sR sin v):
// u and v are the two FACTOR circles of the torus, and they are SMALL circles (radii cos r and
// sin r, not 1). Along BOTH those directions the Hopf map VARIES -- the
// measured spread is 1.414, the largest possible -- so in the labyrinth NO
// coordinate line is a fiber: the fibers are the (1,1) DIAGONALS of that
// grid.
// Here instead the substitution phi -> phi + sfib "straightens" the diagonals onto
// the lines u = const: the point SET is identical (1600 points out of 1600 coincide
// with the labyrinth tube around C34), but the grid is rotated so that
// u runs along the fiber. Visible consequence: the wireframe lines at constant v
// ARE the Hopf fibers -- great circles of S^3 (center at the origin,
// radius 1.000000), Villarceau circles in stereographic projection -- and you can
// see that two distinct fibers are LINKED (linking number 1).
// If you want the "labyrinth" grid just remove the + sfib from the first two
// components: the surface does not change, only the way it is traversed does.
// NB: the variable can NOT be called 's': that name is reserved for the
// slider constant S, which the engine injects as `float s` into every script
// (glwidget.cpp, generateGlslHelperVars) -> "'s' : redefinition".
float sfib = u;
float phi = v;

// NB: the validator in inputvalidator.cpp requires the literal form of the
// return with vec3 or vec4 opened right after it: assigning to a variable and
// returning that is REJECTED. It also counts the parentheses over the whole text,
// COMMENTS INCLUDED: do not write unbalanced ones here.
return vec4(
    cr * cos(phi + sfib),
    cr * sin(phi + sfib),
    sr * cos(sfib),
    sr * sin(sfib)
);
